A||||3||peterh||ignition switch question||||1||
Z||000000||peterh||05-12-2000||08:39 AM||||The ignition switch on my 86 se started acting funny yesterday for the first time. I had to really twist the key switch much further than I normally do to get the starter to crank. Is this switch a bear to replace if it goes? The car has 121,000 miles on but otherwise starts great every time, even after sitting for a month at a time.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Giles||05-12-2000||09:04 AM||||I've had to clean out the ignition switch on my 84 a couple of times.  All you have to do is unbolt the steering wheel from under the dash.  I don't know how it is on an 86, but i    ume it'd be in the same place as mine.  it should be on top of the steering column after you drop it down.  Just take it off and clean it or whatever and put it back in.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-12-2000||09:53 AM||||If it's an automatic there is an interlock cable to remove. Be careful with this cable. Follow the cave link at the bottom of this page to read more about this cable and the steering column.<P>Often if you have to trun the key farther than normal you also need to check the gears and links, especially in the tilt column. <P>In the tilt column. The rack gear that the key turns against has a loop on the end that connects to the link that actually pushes the switch. This loop has a habit of breaking or bending when old.<P>If you replace the switch and the key still gives you trouble then this linkage has a problem. It can be fixed, but is time consuming and you need steering wheel and lock plate tools.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||peterh||05-12-2000||01:09 PM||||Yes the car is an auto and with tilt column. Thanks Theogre, I printed out your notes on the column repair. Hopefully I can repair this myself, I do not have any Fiero expert shops here on Long Island, New York that I know about.<P>||||reg||1
